        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The identification and measurement of toxic volatile organic compounds and airborne pathogens are of critical concern not only to manned space flight, but to all Americans. There are numerous examples where sensors for these materials would be extremely useful. The bioterrorism attacks of 2001 were the result of exposure to an airborne pathogen, anthrax. Many rescue workers at the World Trade Center site suffer from respiratory problems, the result of prolonged exposure to toxic volatile organic compounds and smoke produced by the burning debris. Accounts of office workers becoming ill from toxins and pathogens in building ventilation systems ("sick building syndrome") have frequently occurred over the last two decades. Unfortunately, in all these cases, there are no rapid and accurate methods for quantifying airborne toxins and pathogens. This project sought to create fast-response multianalyte sensors for these materials using biological inspired methods.
<p>
The investigator’s proposed to design and prototype biosensor array systems capable of optically detecting and identifying a wide variety of airborne trace organic, potentially toxic agents. These compounds include ketones, aldehydes, alcohols, alkanes, and halocarbons. This approach to the problem combines basic biological and materials science with novel microfabrication techniques. Investigators sought to immobilize fluorophore-labeled biorecognition molecules (apoenzymes) within novel poly(ethylene glycol) based gel materials that maintain protein conformation and thus biological function, and that can be patterned via photolithography or microcontact printing. These gels will be designed to allow partition of the agent from the gas phase into the gel where it can bind to the recognition molecule. To achieve optical detection, a fluorophore-modified ligand analogous to the agent of interest was immobilized in the gel. In the absence of the agent, the ligand and recognition molecule was bind, resulting in fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET) between the respective fluorophores. When the agent is present, the ligand was displaced competitively, FRET will cease, and the fluorescence in the gel increase. By micropatterning an array of these gels with different recognition molecules, multiple analytes can be simultaneously detected either directly or via pattern recognition techniques. This project focused on the development of a fluorescent microsensor array for the following six model analytes: methanol, isopropanol, acetone, formaldehyde, chloroform, and toluene. Once patterned into an array via photolithography, the changes in FRET upon exposure to gaseous analytes were monitored using a CCD camera. The creation of such an arrays would represent a unique, specific, sensitive biologically-inspired method of gaseous agent detection in manned vehicles. Develop optical sensor arrays for volatile organic compounds, using biological recognition molecules to achieve recognition.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Gel microstructures based upon poly(ethylene glycol) diacrylates, dimethacrylates, and tetraacrylates patterned photolithographically on silicon or glass substrates were created and characterized. A silicon/silicon dioxide surface was treated with 3-(trichlorosilyl)propyl methacrylate to form a self-assembled monolayer (SAM) with pendant acrylate groups.  SAM presence on the surface was verified using ellipsometry and time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry. A solution containing an acrylated or methacrylated poly(ethylene glycol) derivative and a photoinitiator (2,2-dimethoxy-2-phenyl-acetophenone) was spin-coated onto the treated substrate, exposed to 365 nm ultraviolet light through a photomask and developed with either toluene, water, or supercritical CO2. As a result of this process, three-dimensional, cross-linked PEG gel microstructures were immobilized on the surface.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Diameters of cylindrical array members were varied from 600 um to 7 um by the use of different photomasks, while height varied from 3 to 12 um, depending on the molecular weight of the PEG macromer. In the case of 7 um diameter elements as many as 400 elements were reproducibly generated in a 1 mm2 square pattern. The resultant gel patterns were solvent-swollen for as long as three weeks without delamination from the substrate. In addition, micropatterning of different molecular weights of PEG was demonstrated. PEG gel microstructures were also created and arrayed using a microspotting robot, similar to that used to created DNA microarrays. Using this micropatterning technology, arrays of gels capable of detecting multiple analytes may be created without repeated lithographic patterning procedures. We demonstrated that these gel microstructures could be integrated with microfluidic systems for sample deliver via either a gas or liquid stream.
<p>
